{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A panel of trained non-experts following an explicit checklist is more reliable than a single expert for verifiable tasks because the checklist enforces a 'completeness constraint' that mitigates the expert's susceptibility to 'premature closure'.","counterconditions":["The task complexity exceeds a 'Complexity Threshold' where the checklist cannot encode the necessary systemic dependencies (SCR_010).","The checklist is significantly incomplete (e.g., <80% coverage), making the expert's intuitive gap-filling the primary driver of success (SCR_008)."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The checklist transforms the task from a high-dimensional intuitive leap into a series of low-variance binary checks. While the expert may possess superior depth, they are prone to cognitive shortcuts (heuristics) that skip 'obvious' steps. The panel, constrained by the checklist, cannot skip steps, effectively trading peak performance for a higher floor of reliability.","optional_refs":["c24debc9c13b6b069a2611a9c7367570c4b6693f53e4c880d6d815206a495a6d"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["c24debc9c13b6b069a2611a9c7367570c4b6693f53e4c880d6d815206a495a6d"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Genuine reliability in a checklist-driven panel is distinguished from mere compliance by the panel's ability to identify and flag 'logical runtime errors' (contradictions) within the checklist itself.","counterconditions":["The checklist is perfectly logically consistent, leaving no 'runtime errors' to detect.","The panel is trained to prioritize checklist adherence over logical consistency (hyper-compliance)."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"If the panel is merely complying, they will attempt to execute contradictory checklist items literally, resulting in an inconsistent state or a 'lucky' correct answer. A reliable panel acts as a logical processor that halts or flags the checklist when a requirement contradicts the observed data or another requirement (SCR_002, SCR_006).","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The distinction between reliability and compliance can be isolated via a 'Perturbation Test' involving the introduction of a decoy checklist item that is logically irrelevant to the verifiable outcome.","counterconditions":["The decoy item is phrased so convincingly that it appears relevant to a trained non-expert.","The panel's training explicitly forbids flagging items in the provided checklist."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"A 'compliant' panel will check off the decoy item to satisfy the process, whereas a 'reliable' panel will flag the decoy as irrelevant or an anomaly (SCR_004, SCR_007). The evidence of reliability is the presence of this 'flag' alongside the correct verifiable outcome.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Expert intuition is not a distinct cognitive mode but a 'compressed checklist'; therefore, the panel's reliability gain is actually a 'decompression' of the same logic, making the primary evidence of reliability the 'Audit Trail'.","counterconditions":["Expert intuition relies on non-linear pattern recognition that cannot be decomposed into a discrete checklist.","The 'Audit Trail' is filled with post-hoc rationalizations rather than the actual decision-making path."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"If intuition is just internalized procedural knowledge (SCR_003), the panel doesn't provide 'better' logic but 'transparent' logic. Reliability is evidenced by the ability to point to the exact checklist step that caught an error, whereas the expert's reliability is opaque and non-reproducible.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.4},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"For tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes, the distinction between 'reliability' and 'compliance' is a category error; if the outcome is correct, the internal state of the processor (compliance vs. reliability) is irrelevant.","counterconditions":["The system must be evaluated across a distribution of tasks, some of which are designed to break the checklist (stress testing).","The 'reliability' definition includes the ability to maintain accuracy under perturbation."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"In a strictly verifiable system, the only metric of value is the binary match with the ground truth. Whether the panel arrived there via mindless adherence to a list or genuine synthesis is a question of 'depth of understanding' (SRC_003), not 'reliability' of judgment.","optional_refs":["e8e5d71cdd4c79167a068c42000085f48acd724b07e1faa4187c12fceeac3a08"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3}],"context_request":{"desired_retrieval_channels":[],"requested_refs":["c24debc9c13b6b069a2611a9c7367570c4b6693f53e4c880d6d815206a495a6d"]},"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"content":"Thinking about the 'Residual Error Profile' (SCR_001). If we have a dataset of 100 cases: Case A (Checklist works), Case B (Checklist fails but Expert wins), Case C (Both fail). The 'reliability' of the panel is the delta between their performance on Case A and their performance on Case B. If they just comply, they fail Case B completely. If they are reliable, they might catch the failure in Case B by flagging the checklist's inadequacy.","why_keep_this":"Connects the decoy/perturbation ideas to a broader statistical profile of error."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Can we define a 'Reliability Coefficient' as the ratio of (Correct Answers) to (Correct Answers minus Decoy Flags)?","related_refs":["SCR_007","SCR_009"]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}